theory.intro:
Distance and midpoint work in three dimensions exactly as they do in two — you just carry the $z$ terms along. The distance squares the differences and the midpoint averages the coordinates.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\draw[dashed,gray] (0,0)--(-1,-1) node[midway,right,font=\footnotesize]{$x_2-x_1$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (-1,-1)--(3,-1) node[midway,below,font=\footnotesize]{$y_2-y_1$};
\draw[dashed,gray] (3,-1)--(3,2) node[midway,right,font=\footnotesize]{$z_2-z_1$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(3,2) node[midway,above left,font=\footnotesize]{$d$};
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[above left,font=\footnotesize] at (0,0) {$A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
\fill (3,2) circle (2pt);
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (3,2) {$B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Average the matching coordinates.

(a)

$\begin{aligned} M&=\left(\frac{-5+4}{2},\frac{2+(-9)}{2}\right)\\&=\left(-\frac12,-\frac72\right) \end{aligned}$

(b)

$\begin{aligned} N&=\left(\frac{-4+8}{2},\frac{7+(-1)}{2},\frac{-2+5}{2}\right)\\&=\left(2,3,\frac32\right) \end{aligned}$

The midpoint divides the interval into two equal lengths. The sketch shows the relationship, rather than a coordinate projection.

